# Line endings. Committed on purpose -- this file, not a per-machine
# core.autocrlf setting, is the source of truth.
#
# Why this exists: with core.autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes, git expected
# to check these files out as CRLF while the site generators write them as LF.
# The index entry could never be reconciled, so `git status` reported 33 root
# HTML files as permanently modified while `git diff` found nothing in them --
# they were byte-for-byte identical to HEAD. That permanently-dirty tree made
# `git pull --rebase` fail on every run of the start_brsr.py tunnel updater.
# See Ops Book Part 21.7.
#
# .gitattributes takes PRECEDENCE over core.autocrlf, and unlike that setting it
# is committed -- so it applies to every clone, including the Linux prod box,
# and survives a fresh clone or a wiped config.
#
# Verified before committing: 1,544 of the 1,545 tracked text blobs are already
# stored with LF, and none are mixed. So these rules match what is already in
# the repo and change no file's content.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.zip binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
*.db binary
*.npz binary
*.keystore binary

# THE ONE EXCEPTION. assets/css/style.css is the only tracked blob stored with
# CRLF (2,900 lines). Normalising it is a real 2,900-line content change to a
# production stylesheet, which is a deliberate decision rather than a side
# effect of adding this file -- so it is excluded to keep this change zero-diff.
# To normalise it later: delete this line, run
#   git add --renormalize assets/css/style.css
# and commit the resulting EOL-only diff on its own.
assets/css/style.css -text
